.\" basht.1 -- the basht additions to bash. Everything inherited is
.\" documented in bash(1); this page covers only what basht adds.
.\"
.\" basht is a modification of GNU bash and is distributed under the
.\" same terms, GPLv3.
.\"
.TH BASHT 1 "2026 August 11" "basht v0.3" "General Commands Manual"
.SH NAME
basht \- bash adapted to a line-multiplexed tagged display
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B basht
[options]
[command_string | file]
.SH COPYRIGHT
basht is a modification of GNU
.BR bash ,
distributed under the same terms, the GNU General Public License
version 3. This page documents only the basht additions; for the
shell itself \(em options, grammar, expansion, builtins, readline,
job control \(em see
.BR bash (1),
which describes basht's behavior exactly.
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B basht
is bash with a multiplexed console. No task ever writes to the
terminal: every task \(em the shell included \(em writes to its own
pseudoterminals, and output reaches the screen as whole lines, each
prefixed with a tag naming who wrote it. Concurrent tasks cannot
interleave mid-line; background output scrolls above the line being
typed, which survives untouched at the bottom of the screen.
.PP
The shell itself is task 0. Its prompt is nothing special: just
task 0's unfinished line, governed by the same rules as every other
task's.
.PP
An interactive shell brings up the multiplexer. Non-interactive
shells \(em
.B basht \-c
commands, scripts,
.B #!
interpreters, cron jobs \(em are untouched and behave exactly like
stock bash.
.SH THE TAG
Every displayed line is prefixed
.BR [name:n:pid:line] :
the command's name, which instance of that name this session
(\fBhello:2\fP is the second hello started), the process id (which
can be killed directly), and the task's own line counter. A mark
may follow the instance number:
.B !
for stderr,
.B *
for shell events (a task moved to a window, a window closed).
.PP
The tag format is set by the shell variable
.SM
.BR PST1 ,
the preamble's parallel to
.SM
.BR PS1 ;
see
.SM
.B "SHELL VARIABLES"
below.
.SH THE CONSOLE AND INPUT
The bottom line of the screen is the console. It is owned by
whichever task last wrote a character on an incomplete line \(em a
line not yet ended with a newline. A task that completes its lines
has them printed above the console; a task with an incomplete line
\fIis\fP the console, and receives the keyboard for as long as it
owns it. A task that prompts \(em foreground or background \(em
takes the console the moment the prompt appears, and gives it back
when its line completes. The shell's prompt obeys the same rule.
.PP
How keystrokes are delivered follows the owning task's own line
discipline, read from the termios of its stdin:
.TP
.B canonical
(ICANON set: programs using
.BR read (2)
on a cooked terminal,
.BR fgets (3),
the
.B read
builtin). Typing continues the task's visible line exactly as echo
on a shared terminal would; backspace edits; Enter ships the typed
text to the task's stdin and completes the displayed line, so the
task's next output starts a fresh line. If the owning task exits or
completes its line with input still pending, the typed text becomes
editable type-ahead on the shell's command line.
.TP
.B raw
(ICANON clear: readline programs \(em nested shells,
.BR ssh (1),
.BR sudo (8)
.BR \-i ,
REPLs such as python and gdb). Keystrokes pass straight through,
escape sequences included, so the task's own editing, history and
completion work natively; its echo paints the input line. While the
task's terminal keeps ISIG,
.B ^C
and
.B ^Z
are delivered to its process group as the tty driver would;
with ISIG off they are forwarded as bytes.
.PP
A command run in the foreground holds the keyboard like stock bash,
whether or not it prompts, and background tasks keep printing
through the whole time.
.SH TASK CYCLING
.B Alt\-Left
and
.B Alt\-Right
cycle the console through the inputting tasks: every task currently
mid-line, plus the shell, in a ring \(em at the prompt and during a
foreground command alike (the foreground task is always in the
ring). Selection is a forced instance of the ownership rule: at the
prompt, the next task to write on an incomplete line takes the
console back; during a foreground command the selection holds until
the selected task goes away.
.PP
A task cycled away from keeps its half-typed input line and shows
it again when reselected. Selecting the shell during a foreground
command turns typing into a visible type-ahead line for the next
prompt:
.B Up
and
.B Down
browse the shell history on it, and Enter queues the line to run
when the prompt returns.
.SH FULL-SCREEN PROGRAMS
A task that enables the terminal's alternate screen (vim, less,
htop) is detected at its first escape sequence and moved into its
own terminal window; the console never stops multiplexing. The
window tracks resizes, and closing it hangs up the task. The
command used to open the window is taken from
.SM
.B BASHT_TERMINAL
when set; otherwise the common terminal emulators are probed for on
.BR PATH .
If the window fails to open, the task stays on the tagged console
with nothing lost.
.PP
Escape sequences never reach the real terminal from a task on the
console: they are interpreted against the task's own line and
otherwise discarded.
.SH BUILTINS
basht adds two builtins to those described in
.BR bash (1):
.TP
\fBfeed\fP \fIname\fP[:\fIn\fP] \fItext\fP ...
Write \fItext\fP (the remaining arguments joined by single spaces),
followed by a newline, to the standard input of the named task,
without foregrounding it. Tasks are addressed by their display tag;
the :\fIn\fP instance may be omitted when exactly one task with
that name is alive. Returns success unless the spec names no live
task, or more than one and :\fIn\fP was omitted.
.TP
\fBclear\fP [\fB\-x\fP]
The operator's clear: wipes the screen \(em and the scrollback,
unless
.B \-x
\(em and redraws the console. Tasks cannot clear the screen
themselves; that includes
.B clear
run in the background, whose escape sequences are filtered like any
task output. In non-interactive shells and subshells the builtin
writes the ANSI clear sequence to standard output, which is stock
.BR clear (1)
behavior.
.SH SHELL VARIABLES
.TP
.B PST1
The tag template, re-read for every line displayed. Fields:
.B \en
the program name,
.B \ei
the instance number (the stream mark rides just after it),
.B \et
the task process id,
.B \el
the line number;
.B \e\e
a backslash; anything else is literal. Unset, the format is
equivalent to
.PP
.RS
.nf
PST1='[\en:\ei:\et:\el]'
.fi
.RE
.IP
which renders the standard tag. A nonempty tag is followed by one
separating space; an empty
.SM
.B PST1
renders untagged lines.
.TP
.B BASHT_LEVEL
Incremented and exported at multiplexer startup, like
.SM
.BR SHLVL .
A basht started beneath another basht's console (where its terminal
is not a controlling tty it can own) sees it and runs plain, as a
well-behaved raw-mode task of the outer shell, rather than
double-multiplexing. A basht in its own terminal window multiplexes
normally.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B BASHT_TERMINAL
The command prefix used to open a terminal window for a full-screen
task, split on blanks (for example
.BR "gnome\-terminal \-\-" ,
.BR "ptyxis \-\-" ,
.BR "konsole \-e" ).
When unset, the first of gnome\-terminal, ptyxis, konsole,
xfce4\-terminal, kitty, alacritty, foot, wezterm or xterm found on
.B PATH
is used, with the flags each needs.
.TP
.B BASHT_DEBUG
A file name; when set, basht appends a trace of everything touching
the input path, for debugging keystroke routing.
.SH NOTES
A sole command (not part of a pipeline) runs in its own session
with its input pty as controlling terminal, so
.B /dev/tty
resolves to the task's own pty: pagers
.RB ( man ,
.BR less ),
password prompts
.RB ( ssh ,
.BR sudo )
and
.BR getpass (3)
work on the console. Pipeline stages share the job's process group
and have no controlling terminal.
.PP
Nested interactive shells still run without job control: bash
locates its job-control terminal through standard error, which is
a separate capture pty, so
.BR fg ,
.BR bg ,
and
.B ^Z
inside a nested shell do not function, and bash prints its standard
warning at startup. A shell that needs job control belongs in its
own window.
.PP
Task instance numbers are basht's own and never reset during a
session; they are not bash's
.B %n
job numbers.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR bash (1),
.BR pty (7),
.BR stty (1)
.PP
The theory of operation
.RB ( basht.theops ),
the design specification
.RB ( tsh_spec.md ),
and the adaptation notes
.RB ( basht_plan.md )
in the source distribution.
